.\" Title: vrunner-stop-command
.\" Author: vrunner contributors
.\" Manual section: 1
.\" Process this file with
.\"   groff -man -Tascii vrunner-stop-command.1

.TH VRUNNER\-STOP\-COMMAND 1 "2025" "vrunner" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
vrunner\-stop\-command \- stop a running command by PID or name

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vrunner
\fBstop\-command\fR [\fITARGET\fR] [\fB\-\-interactive\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
Stop a specific running command by its OS PID, command name, or command name with arguments. This sends a graceful kill request (SIGINT followed by SIGKILL after the configured \fB\-\-exit\-timeout\fR) to the command, allowing it to clean up resources before termination. This is the CLI equivalent of \fBPOST /api/commands/:id/kill\fR.

When no target is given and exactly one command is running across all instances, that command is stopped automatically. When no target is given and multiple commands are running, a list of all commands is displayed with instructions to specify which one to stop.

Target matching follows a three-round strategy with increasing looseness. A match from an earlier round always wins:
.RS
.IP 1. 4
Exact match: \fBname\fR equals the target, or the full display string (\fBname arg1 arg2\fR) equals the target.
.IP 2. 4
Prefix match on full string: the full display string starts with the target.
.IP 3. 4
Prefix match on name: the command name starts with the target.
.RE

If multiple commands match in any round, all matches are listed and an error suggests using a PID to disambiguate. If the target is numeric, it is treated as an OS PID and resolved directly via the instance's command list.

.SH ARGUMENTS

.TP
.B TARGET
OS PID (numeric), command name, or full "name args" string. If the target contains spaces, quote it: \fB"vim notes.txt"\fR. If omitted and exactly one command is running, that command is stopped automatically. When \fB\-\-interactive\fR is used, this argument can be omitted.

.SH OPTIONS

.TP
.BR \-\-interactive
Present a numbered list of running commands and let the user select
which ones to stop by typing space-separated numbers (e.g. "1 3 5").
Requires a terminal (stdin must be a TTY).

.SH EXAMPLES

.PP
Stop by PID:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner stop-command 5678\fR
Command with PID 5678 stopped on instance 12345 (PID 12345)
.fi
.RE

.PP
Stop by name:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner stop-command htop\fR
Command with PID 5678 stopped on instance 12345 (PID 12345)
.fi
.RE

.PP
Stop by name and args (quoted):
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner stop-command "vim notes.txt"\fR
Command with PID 5679 stopped on instance 12345 (PID 12345)
.fi
.RE

.PP
Stop the only running command (target omitted):
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner stop-command\fR
Command with PID 5678 stopped on instance 12345 (PID 12345)
.fi
.RE

.PP
Equivalent curl:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB# Resolve PID to command ID, then kill:\fR
curl -s -X POST "http://localhost:9090/api/commands/abc123/kill" \e
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \e
  -d '{}'
.fi
.RE

.SH EXIT STATUS

.TP
.B 0
Command stopped successfully.

.TP
.B 1
No matching command found or stop request failed.

.SH SEE ALSO

